[58.798617, "o", "========================================\r\n\r\nQID: near-miss-04f3afa658fdef\r\nDRUG: 12HR Allergy Relief\r\nQUESTION: The 12HR Allergy Relief label discusses warnings. Does it provide a supported recommendation for pharmacogenomic testing requirement? Answer NOT_FOUND if the label does not support it, and cite the section you searched.\r\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n[PASSAGE_0000]\r\nUses\r\ntemporarily relieves these symptoms due to hay fever or other upper respiratory allergies:\r\n- runny nose\r\n- sneezing\r\n- itchy, watery eyes\r\n- itching of the nose or throat\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0001]\r\nDo not use if you have ever had an allergic reaction to this product or any of its ingredients.\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0002]\r\nAsk a doctor before use if you have kidney disease.  Your doctor should determine if you need a different dose.\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0003]\r\n- do not take more than directed\r\n- do not take at the same time as aluminum or magnesium antacids\r\n- do not take with fruit juices\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0004]\r\nStop use and ask a doctor if an allergic reaction to this product occurs.  Seek me\r\n========================================\r\n\r\nQID: near-miss-0c6ec72416151f\r\nDRUG: Acamprosate Calcium\r\nQUESTION: The Acamprosate Calcium label discusses renal impairment. Does it provide a supported recommendation for severe hepatic impairment? Answer NOT_FOUND if the label does not support it, and cite the section you searched.\r\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n[PASSAGE_0002]\r\n2.1 Dosage in Renal Impairment\r\nFor patients with moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance of 30 to 50 mL/min), a starting dose of one 333 mg tablet taken three times daily is recommended. Acamprosate calcium delayed-release tablets are contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance of ≤30 mL/min) .\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0005]\r\n4.2 Severe Renal Impairment\r\nAcamprosate calcium is contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance of ≤30 mL/min) .\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0006]\r\n5.1 Renal Impairment\r\nTreatment with acamprosate calcium in patients with moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance of 30 to 50 mL/min) requires a dose reduction . Acamprosate calcium is contraindicated in patients w\r\n========================================\r\n\r\nQID: near-miss-bd69d0bfb1fc7d\r\nDRUG: Acamprosate Calcium\r\nQUESTION: The Acamprosate Calcium label discusses pregnancy. Does it provide a supported recommendation for breastfeeding dose adjustment? Answer NOT_FOUND if the label does not support it, and cite the section you searched.\r\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n[PASSAGE_0002]\r\n2.1 Dosage in Renal Impairment\r\nFor patients with moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance of 30 to 50 mL/min), a starting dose of one 333 mg tablet taken three times daily is recommended. Acamprosate calcium delayed-release tablets are contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance of ≤30 mL/min) .\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0005]\r\n4.2 Severe Renal Impairment\r\nAcamprosate calcium is contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance of ≤30 mL/min) .\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0006]\r\n5.1 Renal Impairment\r\nTreatment with acamprosate calcium in patients with moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance of 30 to 50 mL/min) requires a dose reduction . Acamprosate calcium is contraindicated in patients with\r\n========================================\r\n\r\nQID: near-miss-d4ca1884aa8ab4\r\nDRUG: Acamprosate Calcium\r\nQUESTION: The Acamprosate Calcium label discusses adverse reactions. Does it provide a supported recommendation for therapeutic drug monitoring target range? Answer NOT_FOUND if the label does not support it, and cite the section you searched.\r\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n[PASSAGE_0002]\r\n2.1 Dosage in Renal Impairment\r\nFor patients with moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance of 30 to 50 mL/min), a starting dose of one 333 mg tablet taken three times daily is recommended. Acamprosate calcium delayed-release tablets are contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance of ≤30 mL/min) .\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0005]\r\n4.2 Severe Renal Impairment\r\nAcamprosate calcium is contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance of ≤30 mL/min) .\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0006]\r\n5.1 Renal Impairment\r\nTreatment with acamprosate calcium in patients with moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance of 30 to 50 mL/min) requires a dose reduction . Acamprosate calcium is contraindicat\r\n========================================\r\n"]
[58.798717, "o", "\r\nQID: near-miss-52e5f8cac33c50\r\nDRUG: Acamprosate Calcium\r\nQUESTION: The Acamprosate Calcium label discusses pediatric. Does it provide a supported recommendation for geriatric maximum cumulative lifetime dose? Answer NOT_FOUND if the label does not support it, and cite the section you searched.\r\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n[PASSAGE_0002]\r\n2.1 Dosage in Renal Impairment\r\nFor patients with moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance of 30 to 50 mL/min), a starting dose of one 333 mg tablet taken three times daily is recommended. Acamprosate calcium delayed-release tablets are contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance of ≤30 mL/min) .\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0005]\r\n4.2 Severe Renal Impairment\r\nAcamprosate calcium is contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance of ≤30 mL/min) .\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0006]\r\n5.1 Renal Impairment\r\nTreatment with acamprosate calcium in patients with moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance of 30 to 50 mL/min) requires a dose reduction . Acamprosate calcium is contraindicated in \r\n========================================\r\n\r\nQID: near-miss-5b5cb6a5249660\r\nDRUG: Acclean Chlorhexidine Gluconate 0.12% Oral Rinse\r\nQUESTION: The Acclean Chlorhexidine Gluconate 0.12% Oral Rinse label discusses pregnancy. Does it provide a supported recommendation for breastfeeding dose adjustment? Answer NOT_FOUND if the label does not support it, and cite the section you searched.\r\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n[PASSAGE_0000]\r\nuntitled\r\nACCLEAN is an oral rinse containing 0.12% chlorhexidine gluconate (1,1’-hexamethylene bis 5-(p-chlorphenyl) biguanide]di-D-gluconate) in a base containing water, 11.6% alcohol, glycerin, PEG-40 sorbitan diisostearate, flavor, sodium saccharin and FD&C Blue No. 1. Chlorhexidine gluconate product is a near neutral solution (pH range 5-7). Chlorhexidine gluconate is a salt of chlorhexidine and gluconic acid. Its chemical structure is:\r\n........................................\r\n[PASSAGE_0001]\r\nACCLEAN chlorhexidine gluconate oral rinse provides antimicrobial activity during oral rinsing. The clinical significance of chlorhexidine gluconate oral rinse’s antimicrobial activities is not clear. Microbiological sampling of plaque has shown a general reducti\r\n"]
